      "path": "CompTIA-A-Core-1-220-1201-Certification-Training-Exam-Prep/Section 1/Critical_Lectures_Notes/Doc_format_with Word_2/Section8_Lecture2_Malware_Types_Attack_Vectors_and_Mitigation_Deep_Dive_Guide.docx",
      "package_path": "study_assets/CompTIA-A-Core-1-220-1201-Certification-Training-Exam-Prep/Section 1/Critical_Lectures_Notes/Doc_format_with Word_2/Section8_Lecture2_Malware_Types_Attack_Vectors_and_Mitigation_Deep_Dive_Guide.docx",
      "content": "CompTIA A+ (Core Security Foundations)\nSection 8 - Lecture 2\nMalware Types, Attack Vectors & Mitigation - Deep-Dive Professional Guide\nThis document is a student-facing, professional reference that explains common malware types, how attacks are delivered, and how systems are protected as tested in the CompTIA A+ exams. It focuses on behavior-based identification and exam-safe mitigation strategies.\n1. Understanding Malware\nMalware is any software designed to harm systems, steal data, or disrupt operations. Attackers use different malware types depending on their objectives.\n2. Viruses and Worms\nViruses require user action to spread, often attaching to legitimate files. Worms spread automatically without user interaction.\nUnderstanding propagation behavior is critical for exam identification.\n3. Trojans and Backdoors\nTrojans disguise themselves as legitimate software. Backdoors provide unauthorized remote access to systems.\n4. Ransomware and Cryptomalware\nRansomware encrypts data and demands payment for recovery.\nRegular backups are the most effective defense.\n5. Spyware and Adware\nSpyware secretly collects user information. Adware displays unwanted advertisements and may track behavior.\n6. Rootkits and Fileless Malware\nRootkits hide malicious activity at deep system levels.\nFileless malware operates in memory, making detection difficult.\n7. Common Attack Vectors\nAttack vectors include email attachments, malicious links, compromised websites, and removable media.\n8. Malware Mitigation Techniques\nMitigation includes antivirus software, system updates, backups, and user awareness.\nDefense in depth provides layered protection.\n9. Exam-Focused Malware Identification\nCompTIA exam questions often describe symptoms such as encryption, pop-ups, or slow performance.\nStudents must match symptoms to malware type.\nFinal Guidance for Students\nUnderstanding malware behavior and mitigation prepares students to recognize attacks quickly and respond appropriately in both exam and real-world environments.",
      "excerpt": "CompTIA A+ (Core Security Foundations) Section 8 - Lecture 2 Malware Types, Attack Vectors & Mitigation - Deep-Dive Professional Guide This document is a student-facing, professional reference that explains common malware types, how attacks are delivered, and how systems are protected as tested in the CompTIA A+ exams. It focuses on behavior-based identification and exam-safe mitigation strategies"
