      "content": "1\nChapter 3\n2\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 3.01\nWhat is a CPU?\nN/A\n3\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 3.02\nModern CPUs\n• The ca pa bility of a CPU is mea sured via clock speed and cores\n• Modern CP Us support advanced features such as multi -cores\n• ARM chips operate using a R educed Instruction Set Computing (RISC) methodology\n• Accelerated Processing Units (APUs) are CPUs with gra phics cards built in\n•\n4\nLower 3rds\nCPU - Executes system and program \ninstructions\nInstruction Set Architectures (ISAs) \nCommon architectures: x86 / x64\nComplex Instruction Set Computer (CISC)\nReduced Instruction Set Computing (RISC)\n5\nLower 3rds\nx86 by Intel\nx64 by AMD\nThe ARM CPU is based on the RISC \narchitecture\nCores and clock speed. \nClock speed - measured by number of \ntasks done in one second\n6\nComplex Instruction \nSet Computer (CISC) \nLarge complex set of built -in \ninstructions\n• Several of which are multiple cycle \noperations\n• Versatile working with hardware\n7\nReduced Instruction \nSet Computing (RISC) \nSmaller and simpler built -in \ninstruction set\n• Focused on efficiency and speed\n8\nx64 x86\n64-bit ISA 32-bit ISA\n16 exabytes of \nRAM\n4 GB of RAM\nSupports 32 & \n64-bit systems\nSupports 32-\nbit systems\nFaster Slower\nComparing x64/x86\n9\nCPU measurements\nCPU cores - multiple \nprocessors in one chip\nClock speed - number of \ntasks a CPU can do in one \nsecond\nClock cycle - CPUs speed \nrepresented as the \nnumber of clock cycles \nper second\n10\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 3.03\n32-Bit Vs. 64-Bit Computing \nx86, x86-64, x64, and IA -32 all support different I nstruction Set A rchitectures (I SAs)\nAlmost all modern hardware and software supports 64 -bit systems and are backwards compa tible with 32 -bit \nsystems\nx64 systems offer greater data handling, improved graphics performance, and better security\n11\nLower 3rds\nDifferences of 32-bit/64-bit = amount of data \naddressed in RAM\nNearly all computers in the past 5 years have \nbeen 64-bit systems\nLinux distros still have 32-bit versions\n64-bit system can address and move a lot more \ndata\n12\n32-bit or 64-bit?\nCheck if your Windows system is a \n32 or 64 bit system - access the \nAbout option on the Settings app \nSystem page to display the \nDevice specifications.\n13\n32-bit or 64-bit?\nOn a MacOS system, enter the \ncommand:\nsysctl -n \nmachdep.cpu.brand_string\nExample:\n\n14\n32-bit or 64-bit?\nOn a Linux distro, enter the \ncommand: lscpu \nat a command prompt\n\n15\nEpisode 3.04\nChoosing the Right CPU\nCPUs designed for laptops \ncome with features to help \nwith power consumption \nand battery usage\nHigher-end CPUs offer more \ncores and a faster clock \nspeed\nIntensive tasks such as 3D \ngaming and video editing \nbenefit from CPUs with \nextra cores\nSome CPUs are unlocked \nand can be overclocked\n16\nLower 3rds\nCentral processing unit (CPU) \nCPU Requirements\nPurpose or application \nDesktop CPUs - more horsepower\nLaptop CPUs - better power efficiencies\n17\nInitial Considerations\n• What is the primary purpose of the \nPC?\n• What platform is best for your \nneeds: \n• desktop \n• laptop\n• hand-held\n18\nLifespan of a \ncomputer system\n• Heat\n• Accidentals\n• Obsolescence\n• Vulnerability\n19\nAdditional criteria\n• Budget\n• Manufacturer\n• Motherboard\n20\nLower 3rds\nCPU cost range - $39 to under $10k\nLand Grid Array or LGA socket \n21\nMotherboards\nConsider CPU \nmounting socket\nIntel motherboards\nAMD motherboards\n22\nMotherboards\nIntel motherboards have a Land \nGrid Array (LGA) socket \n\n23\nMotherboards\nAMD motherboards have a Pin \nGrid Array (PGA) socket\n\n24\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 3.05\nCPU Generations and Architecture\n220-1101\n3.4 Given a scenario, i nstall and configure motherboards, central processing units (CPUs), and add -on cards. \n• Motherboard compatibility\n• CPU sockets \n• ADM and Intel\n25\nLower 3rds\nOBJ - Motherboard compatibility \nOBJ - CPU sockets \nOBJ - AMD and Intel\nUnderstanding the CPU can help you identify \nthe right one\nCPU name components - Intel and AMD\nThe generation is a good indicator of a CPU's \nage\n26\nLower 3rds\nSeries: Core and Ryzen\nPerformance tier: 9/i9, 7, 8, i5, or i7\n12900K of the Intel CPU and the 5900X \nof the AMD CPU\n27\nCPU Name components\nIntel and AMD:\nSeries name and CPU tier\nInitial name - identify line \nof processor (Core/Ryzen)\nNumber after series is the \nperformance tier\n28\nSeries name\nIdentifies the specific \nline of processors \n29\nRight fit\nUnderstanding the branding and \nnomenclature on a CPU can help \nyou to know whether a CPU is \nthe right one\n• Also helps you find what meets \nyour specific requirements\n30\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 3.06\nCPU Cooling\nHeat sinks use metal fins and pipes to pas sively transfer heat\nThermal paste and pad s are both used to fill in gaps and pro vide better thermal conductivity between CPU and heat sink\nThere are numerous s izes of fans and radiators to choose from\nLiquid coo ling h as higher thermal transfer capabilities th an air-cooling\n31\nLower 3rds\nOBJ - Cooling\nKeeping CPU within temperature tolerance = \nExtends life/assures performance\nAirflow is important to keep the CPU cool\nDust\nDust or particles can prevent operation of fans \nand trap heat\nDefective fans - Damaged system or fan can \nreduce cooling\n32\nLower 3rds\nCPU or GPU\nApplications\nOBJ - Fans\nCooling fans - fundamental component of an air \ncooling system\ncubic feet per minute (CFM)\nOBJ - Heat sinks\nIntegrated Heat Spreader (HIS)\n33\nLower 3rds\nHeat sink absorbs heat from CPU by \nthermal paste\nOBJ - Thermal paste/pads\nThermal paste is applied between the CPU and a \nheat sink to fill any air gaps\nOBJ - Liquid \nLiquid cooling systems circulate coolant to absorb \nthe heat and then exude the heat to cooling \nelements\n34\nCooling Fans\nFans move warm \nair out and pull \ncool air into \nthe case\n35\nHeat Sinks\nAbsorbs heat away from the \nof a CPU through the \nthermal paste\n\n36\nHeat Pipes\nAbsorb the heat through its base \ncool it through pipes filled with \nwater or alcohol \n\n37\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 3.07\nInstalling and Troubleshooting a \nCPU\nAlways use ESD prevention metho ds when handling CPU s\nPin Grid Array (PGA) an d Land Grid Array (LGA) are the two mo st co mmon types of CPU s ockets\nThe Zero-insertio n force (ZI F) mechanism is used to secure the CPU into the motherboard’s CPU s ocket\nWhen tro u bleshoo ting a non-functio nal CPU , first check all co nnections and make sure the fan, heat sink, and CPU itself are s eated \nproperly before proceed ing\n38\nLower 3rds\nOBJ - Motherboards have specific manufacturer’s \ndesigns\n2 primary manufacturers: Intel - AMD\nLGA socket format - Intel CPUs\nPGA socket format - AMD CPUs\nZIF socket designed for easy insertion/extraction\n39\nLower 3rds\n1. Identify the problem\n2. Question the obvious\n3. Test the theory\n4. Establish a plan of action\n5. Verify functionality \n6. Document the findings \n40\nCompTIA \ntroubleshooting\n1. Identify the problem\n2. Question the obvious\n3. Test the theory\n4. Establish a plan of action\n5. Verify functionality \n6. Document the findings \n\n41\n\n42\n\n43\n\n44",
