      "content": "1\nEssential Peripherals\nChapter 10\n2\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.01\nUSB Standards\nN/A\n3\nUSB Standards \nand Speeds\nStandard Maximum \nSpeed\nUSB 1.0 1.5 Mbps\nUSB 1.1 12 Mbps\nUSB 2.0 480 Mbps\nUSB 3.0 5 Gbps\nUSB 3.1 Gen 1 5 Gbps\nUSB 3.1 Gen 2 10 Gbps\n4\nUSB Standards and Speeds\nStandard Maximum \nSpeed\nUSB 1.0 1.5 Mbps\nUSB 1.1 12 Mbps\nUSB 2.0 480 Mbps\nUSB 3.0 5 Gbps\nUSB 3.1 Gen 1 5 Gbps\nUSB 3.1 Gen 2 10 Gbps\n5\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.02\nUnderstanding USB\nN/A\n6\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.03\nConfiguring USB\nN/A\n7\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.04\nThunder and Lightning\nN/A\n8\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.05\nOptical Media\nShiny optical discs are an excellent media for transporting data and \nare still a very common tool for delivering drivers and configuration \nutilities for hardware. There’s several different optical standards and \nit’s critical to know that a certain optical drive supports a certain \noptical technology. \n9\nLower 3rds\nOBJ - Optical d\nOptical drives use a laser diode to read and write data onto an optical disc\nAn optical drive has five major components: a disc tray, the laser diode, a lens, a s p indle moto r, and the controlling electronics\nOptical drives uses pits and lands to encode its data\nRives\ndisc tray \nConstant Angu lar Velocity (CAV)\nlaser diode - laser beam that senses data \nLens\ncontrolling electronics manages the data tran s fer \npits and lands \nSATA \nDisk - internal/encased storage\nDisc - removable ex ternal storage\n10\nDVD Media Varieties\nCD\nROM\nCD CD\nR RW\n650-700 MB\n11\nDVD Version Capacity\nDVD-5 (12 cm, SS/SL) 4.37 GB, more than two \nhours of video\nDVD-9 (12 cm, SS/DL) 7.95 GB, about four hours \nof video\nDVD-10 (12 cm, DS/SL) 8.74 GB, about four and a \nhalf hours of video\nDVD-18 (12 cm, DS/DL) 15.90 GB, more than eight \nhours of video\n12\nType Size Capacity (single) Capacity (dual layer)\nStandard disc 12 cm 25 GB 50 GB\nMini disc 8 cm 7.8 GB 15.6 Gb\n13\nOptical Drive\nFive major components: \n• Disc tray\n• Laser diode\n• Lens\n• Spindle motor\n• Controlling \nelectronics\n14\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.06\nReaders and Scanners\nN/A\n15\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.07\nCommon Peripherals\nPeripherals allow us to expand our system to support new features \nsuch as printing, sound, and external storage. They also enable a wide \narray of input devices like mice and keyboards. Knowing how to \nidentify each quickly is key to your success in the field and on the \nexam.\n16\nLower 3rds\nUniversal Serial Bus - USB, USB-C, \nmicroUSB, miniUSB\nUSB 1.0/1.1 (USB Type A and B)\nUSB Type C\nmicroUSB\nminiUSB\nLightning\n17\nLower 3rds\nNear-field communication (NFC)\nBluetooth\nTethering/hotspot\nStylus\nHeadset\nSpeakers\n18\nLower 3rds\nWebcam\nTrackpad\nDrawing pad\nTrackpoint\nDocking station\nPort replicator\n19\nUSB Type A and B\nUsed on conversion cables with a \ndifferent connection type on the \nopposite end\nA\nB\n\n20\nUSB Type C\nCommon connections\n• New smartphones \n• Game controllers\n• Microphones\n• Mobile devices\nType C \n\n21\nMicro USB\nConnection is both Type -A / Type-\nB micro versions \nIt is less expensive than USB -C. \nMicro USB\n\n22\nMini USB\nMini-me of a USB-B there are also \nType-A versions\nMini USB connectors are not \ncommonly used\nMini USB-B \n23\nLightning\nApple's proprietary connector \nLike a USB-C, it is reversible \nSimilar in specifications to the \nUSB 3.0 standard\nNewer Apple devices all now use \nthe USB-C connection\nApple Lightning\n\n24\nTrackpad\nOther names\n• Touchpad\n• Glide pad\nUsed to do anything that could \nbe done with a mouse\nCan be embedded or a \nperipheral device\nExternal Touch pad\nInternal Touch pad\n\n25\nDrawing Pad: \nDrawing pads provide a flat \nsurface to draw, sketch, or paint \nusing a stylus\n26\nTrackPoint\nA TrackPoint is a small \nrubber joystick typically \nlocated in the center of \na keyboard\n27\nDocking Station\nAllows a mobile device to \nadd ports it doesn’t have \nConnects multiple \nperipherals through a \nsingle connection \n28\nPort Replicator\nThe port replicator mirrors \nthe PC’s existing ports\nPeripherals stay connected \nwhen the PC is removed\n29\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.08\nWebcams and Videoconferencing\nVideo conferencing software has changed the way we work, \nespecially with more and more companies moving to remote and \nhybrid workplaces. This episode gives a quick demonstration of \nMicrosoft Teams and its screensharing function.\n30\nLower 3rds\nVideoconferencing is a communication \nservice for audio conferencing or \naudio/video conferencing. \nVideoconferencing includes audio and \ndata sharing support. \nVideoconferencing services provide \nscreen shares, messaging, recording, as \nwell as integrated applications. \n31\nLower 3rds\nScreen sharing only software and \nservices are available \nA web cam can be added as a \nperipheral device to virtually any \ncomputer \nExternal webcams are generally \nconnected to the system through a USB \nport. \n32\nVideo Conferencing\nVideo conferencing allows users to meet \nvirtually over the internet\n\n33\nInternal \nWebcam\nCan be an \nembedded device \nthat is built into the \nframing around the \ndisplay screen\n34\nExternal Webcam\nExternal webcams are generally \nconnected to the system through \na USB port\n\n35\nEpisode \ntitle:\nObjective:\nEpisode 10.09\nInstalling and Troubleshooting \nExpansion Cards\nEven though we live in a world of built -to-exact-specification systems, \nyou will eventually need to install and possibly troubleshoot \nexpansion cards. Let’s look at the modern expansion card formats \nand then get hands -on and troubleshoot some common issues.\n36\nLower 3rds\nSound card\nVideo card\nCapture card\nNetwork interface card\n \n37\nLower 3rds\nPCI express is the updated standard for \nthe PCI standard\nA PCI express slot have: 1, 4, 8, 16, or 32 \nlanes\nPCI express slots are used for sound \ncards, video capture cards, network \ninterface cards or NICs, or even RAID \ncards\n \n38\nPCI express slots\nCan be used for:\n• Sound cards\n• Video capture cards\n• Network interface cards (NICs)\n• RAID cards\n39\nPCI (Sound Card)",
